  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Mikhail Jekovich admits that although he and Viktor Petrovich often clashed, Barinov is a good man—so Mikhail kept the pony at a stable and brought his own personal savings. Yet just as they gather nearly the full sum, Elena gets devastating news from Germany.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Мы с твоим отцом не ладили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Но человек он правда хороший.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты всё-таки продал своего коня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, я коня себе оставил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Тимур мне конюшню недалеко нашёл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вот это мои личные сбережения.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам огромное, дорогие ребята.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Елена Павловна, мы нашли деньги!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы собрали почти всю сумму.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лена, а что сейчас случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мне ответили из немецкой клиники.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Операция будет в два раза дороже.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Мы с твоим отцом не ладили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your father and I did not get along.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'we', while 'got along' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Но человек он правда хороший.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But he is truly a good man.' In Russian, the idea of 'but' flows naturally into 'good' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты всё-таки продал своего коня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you sell your horse after all?' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'horse' without extra filler words. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, я коня себе оставил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I kept the horse for myself.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'no' and 'I' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Тимур мне конюшню недалеко нашёл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Timur found a stable for me nearby.' In Russian, the idea of 'Timur' flows naturally into 'to find' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вот это мои личные сбережения.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here, these are my personal savings.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'here' and 'this' in a few short words. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам огромное, дорогие ребята.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you so very much, dear guys.' Katya anchors the line on 'thank you', while 'guys' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Елена Павловна, мы нашли деньги!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Elena Pavlovna, we found the money!' The grammar packs 'Elena' and 'money' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы собрали почти всю сумму.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We collected almost the entire sum.' Notice how Russian expresses 'we' and 'sum' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лена, а что сейчас случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lena, what happened just now?' The grammar packs 'Lena' and 'happened' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мне ответили из немецкой клиники.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They replied to me from the German clinic.' The meaning hinges on 'to me' working together with 'clinic' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Операция будет в два раза дороже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The operation will be twice as expensive.' Notice how Russian expresses 'operation' and 'more expensive' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
